--- At the end of standard input, input[] answers empty text that did not fail — the same
--- value an empty line gives — so a program cannot tell that its input has ended.
---
--- Run with no input:  crow run -i x.co < /dev/null   and   crow run x.co < /dev/null
--- Both modes print
---     true
---     0
---     true
--- Expected (by the spec's table of fallible builtins, "input[] / input[prompt]: Stdin read
--- fails", and as Python's input() raises EOFError and Go's Scanln answers io.EOF): an error
--- value at the end of the input — `false`, with a reason such as `end of input` — so that a
--- loop reading lines can stop. The spec says nothing of the end of the input, so this may be
--- a question for the spec rather than a slip: crow_runtime::core::read_line maps
--- `read_line` → Ok(0) to Ok(""). As it is, a game loop that re-prompts on an empty line
--- spins forever once a scripted input runs out; rosetta/15_puzzle_game.co, 21_game.co,
--- 24_game.co and a_plus_b.co end on an empty line as well as on a failed read.
---
--- Update (19:25): the repository's working tree now answers `Err("end of input")` there
--- (crow_runtime/src/core.rs, uncommitted, citing Rosetta Code's ABC correlation), so this
--- is known. Meanwhile the pinned binary's interpreter still answers '' while a compiled
--- program, which builds against the working tree's runtime, answers the error: the two
--- modes differ at the end of the input until the binary is rebuilt.

first = input[]
log[first.ok]
log[len[first]]
second = input[]
log[second.ok]
